---
// TONE (owner direction): calm and reassuring, never adversarial. The point is that
// the whole process is stressful and Coastal carries it — from the insurance claim
// through the final coat of paint. Do not spell out the insurer-vs-homeowner
// imbalance; that's the reason the page works, not something to say out loud.
//
// LEGAL LINE (spec §6): describing documentation, scope of work, and walking the
// property with the adjuster is standard contractor work. Never state or imply that
// Coastal negotiates, adjusts, or settles the claim itself — under Fla. Stat.
// 489.147 a contractor may not negotiate a claim, and advertising it is unlicensed
// public adjusting (Fla. Stat. 626.854).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/TrustBar.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Insurance Claim Help | Storm & Water Damage Restoration | Coastal Property Restorations"
  description="From the insurance claim to the final coat of paint, Coastal Property Restorations handles the whole process — documentation, scope, and rebuild — for Southwest Florida homeowners."
  breadcrumb="Insurance Claim"
>
  <TrustBar />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 46rem;">
      <h1>From the Insurance Claim to the Final Coat of Paint</h1>
      <p>
        When your home is damaged, the repairs are only part of what lands on you.
        There's the documentation. The adjuster's visit. Working out the scope of
        the work. Scheduling trades. Keeping track of all of it — while you're also
        trying to get your family back to normal.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's a lot to carry, and most people are carrying it for the first time.
        <strong>You don't have to.</strong> One call and we take it from there.
      </p>

      <h2>What we take off your plate</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The documentation.</strong> Photos of every affected area, moisture readings logged daily, and a written record that starts the hour we arrive.</li>
        <li><strong>The scope of work.</strong> A detailed, line-by-line account of what your home actually needs to be put back — nothing overlooked, nothing padded.</li>
        <li><strong>The adjuster's walkthrough.</strong> We're there with the documentation and we go through the repairs with them, so it isn't on you to explain moisture readings or roof decking.</li>
        <li><strong>The rebuild.</strong> The same company that dried it out and documented it does the repairs. No handoffs, no finger-pointing, one point of contact.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it goes</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Call us.</strong> We'll tell you what to do right now and get a crew moving.</li>
        <li><strong>We stop the damage.</strong> Tarping, water extraction, dry-out — documented from the first hour.</li>
        <li><strong>We put the file together.</strong> Full photo record, moisture logs, and the detailed scope of repairs.</li>
        <li><strong>The inspection.</strong> We meet the adjuster at your property and walk the damage with them.</li>
        <li><strong>We rebuild.</strong> Repairs, then a final walkthrough with you and your complete documentation package.</li>
      </ol>

      <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        The single most useful thing you can do is call early — the record that
        protects you starts the moment we walk in.
        <a href={site.phoneHref}>Call {site.phone}</a> and we'll take it from there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQ
    items={[
      { q: 'Who should I call first?', a: 'Call us. We can start protecting the property right away and get everything documented properly from the first hour — that record is what the rest of the process is built on. We will walk you through what comes next so nothing catches you off guard.' },
      { q: 'Do I have to deal with the adjuster myself?', a: 'No. We meet the adjuster at your property with the full documentation and go through the repairs with them. You are welcome to be there, but you will not be the one explaining what the damage takes to fix.' },
      { q: 'What documentation do you provide?', a: 'Photos of every affected area from the first hour forward, daily moisture readings during dry-out, and a detailed line-by-line scope of the repairs. You get the complete package at the end of the job.' },
      { q: 'How long does the whole process take?', a: 'Dry-out is typically 3–5 days, and the rebuild depends on the extent of the damage. We give you a written timeline once the scope is set, and we keep you updated as it moves — no wondering where things stand.' },
      { q: 'What is a deductible?', a: 'The portion of the loss you pay before your coverage picks up the rest. In Florida, hurricane deductibles are often a percentage of your home\'s insured value rather than a flat dollar amount, which surprises a lot of people. If you are not sure how yours works, give us a call and we will help you make sense of it.' },
    ]}
  />

  <CTABand heading="One call. We'll take it from here." />
</Base>
